\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{An axial label on the spin carrier}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Use the axial label in \ref{fdip-016J}, supported on direction
#{c} with distinct equal-period transverse axes #{a,b}.
Let the lifts act on spin fields by the pullback of their
coordinate image and their given operator on #{S}, with trivial
links. The image of #{u_{ab}} fixes the label. Thus on a field
#{y\mapsto\alpha_r(y)v} it acts by #{u_{ab}} on #{v}, as in
\ref{fdip-016D}. Choose #{\zeta=(1+\ui)/\sqrt{2}}. The operator
#{\varepsilon_a\varepsilon_b} squares to #{-\bbI_S}, so over
#{\mathbb C} its eigenspaces at #{\pm\ui} span #{S}. They have
equal positive dimension because the invertible #{\varepsilon_a}
interchanges them by anticommutation and #{S\ne0}. On these
spaces the lifted turn has eigenvalues
#{(1\pm\ui)/\sqrt{2}=\zeta^{\pm1}}. Hence precisely the
degrees #{1,-1} modulo #{8} occur, both of negative central
sign by \ref{fdip-016X}. Reversing axis #{a} also fixes the label. Its determinant is
minus one, whereas every power of this turn preserves orientation.}
